From e258587f64983849231feaa678727911e079a6f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 07:22:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 80/90] usb: dwc3: Tell the UDC core when there is no cable

The USB3 PHY is powered off while no cable is connected, so anything
that touches the endpoints in that state fails. Binding a gadget driver
does exactly that: the UDC core pulls up as soon as a driver binds,
because udc->vbus starts out true and nothing ever says otherwise, and
the pullup runs __dwc3_gadget_start() against the powered off PHY:

  dwc3 fe800000.usb: failed to enable ep0out

Report the connection state with usb_udc_vbus_handler() so the core only
pulls up when there is a cable. That also replaces the
dwc3_gadget_suspend()/dwc3_gadget_resume() pair that was approximating
it: the pullup is what has to follow the cable, and the core already
knows how to do that, including for a driver that binds later while
still disconnected.

usb_udc_vbus_handler() only records the new state and schedules the
pullup change to a workqueue, though, while __dwc3_set_mode() goes
straight on with the teardown: event buffers freed, GCTL.CORESOFTRESET
asserted for 100ms and the USB3 PHY powered off. A soft disconnect
scheduled at unplug races into that window and runs against a core held
in reset with its PHY clocks gone: the endpoint end transfer commands
read back fake completions, the DCTL/DSTS/DEVTEN accesses are lost, and
the controller is left in a state even the DCTL.CSFTRST on the next
plug-in does not recover from. The next session then comes up with a
mute ep0 (host: 'Device not responding to setup address') and a stuck
event interrupt that ends in a softirq lockup and 'irq: nobody cared'.

So change the pullup synchronously with the cable state on top of the
bookkeeping: usb_gadget_disconnect() before touching the event buffers,
the core reset or the PHY power, and usb_gadget_connect() after the
core is set back up. The scheduled work then finds the pullup already
in the requested state and does nothing. Both directions serialize on
the UDC connect lock, so a quick unplug also waits for an in-flight
connect instead of resetting the core under it.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
---
 drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c b/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c
index b1e6178cec7b..7e86dce3d317 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.c
@@ -204,8 +204,20 @@ static void __dwc3_set_mode(struct work_struct *work)
 	 * the runtime PM path does it.
 	 */
 	if (!role_changed && dwc->current_dr_role == DWC3_GCTL_PRTCAP_DEVICE &&
-	    dwc->current_dr_connected) {
-		dwc3_gadget_suspend(dwc);
+	    dwc->current_dr_connected && dwc->gadget) {
+		/*
+		 * usb_udc_vbus_handler() only records the state and schedules
+		 * the pullup change for later, but the controller reset and
+		 * PHY power off below must not run while the gadget is still
+		 * live: the soft disconnect (endpoint end transfer commands,
+		 * DCTL/DEVTEN writes) would then hit a core that is held in
+		 * reset with its PHY clocks gone, be silently lost, and leave
+		 * the controller in a state the next plug-in cannot recover
+		 * from. Stop the gadget synchronously first; the scheduled
+		 * work then finds nothing left to do.
+		 */
+		usb_udc_vbus_handler(dwc->gadget, false);
+		usb_gadget_disconnect(dwc->gadget);
 		dwc3_event_buffers_cleanup(dwc);
 	}
 
@@ -354,7 +366,24 @@ static void __dwc3_set_mode(struct work_struct *work)
 		dwc3_event_buffers_setup(dwc);
 		phy_set_mode(dwc->usb2_generic_phy[0], PHY_MODE_USB_DEVICE);
 		phy_set_mode(dwc->usb3_generic_phy[0], PHY_MODE_USB_DEVICE);
-		dwc3_gadget_resume(dwc);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Tell the UDC core whether there is a cable, so that it only pulls up
+	 * when there is one. Without this it pulls up as soon as a gadget
+	 * driver binds, which starts the endpoints on a USB3 PHY that is
+	 * powered off while disconnected, and ep0 fails to come up.
+	 *
+	 * Perform the connect synchronously as well, instead of leaving it to
+	 * the scheduled vbus work: the pullup then happens strictly after the
+	 * core and event buffer setup above, and a disconnect that follows a
+	 * quick unplug serializes against it on the UDC connect lock instead
+	 * of tearing the controller down under a half-finished connect.
+	 */
+	if (desired_dr_role == DWC3_GCTL_PRTCAP_DEVICE && dwc->gadget) {
+		usb_udc_vbus_handler(dwc->gadget, desired_dr_connected);
+		if (desired_dr_connected)
+			usb_gadget_connect(dwc->gadget);
 	}
 
